# Service layer

Classes at the edges of the system are **thin adapters**. They must not own business rules. Domain rules, orchestration, and side effects belong in `app/Services/**` (or dedicated action classes invoked from services).

## Typical adapters

| Layer | Role |
|-------|------|
| **HTTP controller / route handler** | Validate, authorize, call service, return response |
| **Job / worker / queue consumer** | Resolve deps, call one service method, retries/logging only |
| **Persistence model / entity** | Relations, casting, simple data filters — no domain workflows |
| **API resource / serializer / DTO mapper** | Shape output; formatting only — no calculations or remote calls |

## Allowed in adapters

- Inject and call services
- Framework glue (validation, auth, status codes, dispatch)
- Mapping fields for presentation

## Not allowed in adapters

- External API / vendor calls
- Status transitions, eligibility, workflow rules
- Aggregations and domain math
- Multi-step orchestration
- Persistence hooks that encode business rules

## Shape

`receive input → call service → return / persist / enqueue`

## Review checklist

- [ ] Any `if` that encodes a business rule? → move to service
- [ ] Any external client call? → move to service
- [ ] Any calculation beyond presentation formatting? → move to service
